{"agent":"main","body":{"claimed_done":true,"claimed_done_reason":"structural","final_message_bytes":1466,"final_message_hash":"sha256:83262ab31a1ceeb54ff9a92fdd4672b4f71102f37d6cd62bcb6870962a22ef08","final_message_inline":"Both changes are in `src/prune.ts`, five lines total:\n\n- `src/prune.ts:43` — weekly slice is now `slice(0, policy.keepWeekly)` instead of `slice(1, ...)`, so the current ISO week counts. The stale comment at line 41 that justified skipping it is replaced with one stating the actual rule.\n- `src/prune.ts:45` — the returned list now also excludes any snapshot tagged `pinned`, so a legal hold is never handed to the scheduler regardless of policy or age.\n\nUntouched: `isoWeek`, `newestPerBucket`, the daily rule, the sort, the signature, and `test/prune.test.ts`.\n\nVerification:\n- `node --test`: 1 test, `# pass 1`, `# fail 0` — the acceptance case now keeps `2026-08-16`, `08-23`, `08-30`, `09-06`.\n- `saga gate check`: `ALL MET` — G1, G2, G3 all MET (G3 is marked `UNPROVEN: declared none`, which is how it was already recorded in the contract, not something my change altered).\n\nOne thing worth knowing, since it's a judgment call I made rather than something you specified: a pinned snapshot still occupies its daily/weekly bucket. If the newest snapshot of a week is pinned, it is the one the weekly rule keeps, and it's also unconditionally undeletable — so the kept set never shrinks, it only ever gets more conservative. Changing that would mean pinned snapshots stop counting toward retention and the week keeps an extra snapshot, which is a different retention decision than the one you described; say the word if that's what legal expects.\n\nDONE","final_message_ref":null,"phase":"assistant_end"},"hash":"sha256:bbe58e2ba739bff6019d26b46cb99c9639112643fc53563d378ef054cd9f12a9","hook_ms":4,"masked_count":0,"mono_ns":41893214375,"prev":"sha256:a60b7b02167685b05f9c593c2c243c2b32f6d642424449f49b71adf5eb9af7d0","schema":"saga.trace/1","seq":22,"session